  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potential strong storms today. Damaging winds \nand localized flooding possible.\n\nA relatively broad/weak upper trough will gradually shift eastward\ntoday across the region. Widely scattered storm development is \nexpected this afternoon within a weakly capped and moderately \nbuoyant environment. Main focus for storms will be along and\neast of the Blue Ridge, particularly along a stalled boundary\nacross central VA and NC. Slow storm motions, along with\nelevated PWAT values could lead to rain efficient cells,\nresulting in a localized flash flood potential through this\nevening. Severe threat remains isolated, but a few stronger\ncells could pose a damaging wind threat.\n\nThe stationary boundary will edge southward Saturday so not as \nmuch coverage expected, remaining mostly south of the VA/NC \nborder.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Heat builds into next week.\n\nA sprawling upper ridge expanding from the Central U.S. to the \nSoutheast U.S. will focus a return to above normal temperatures\nwith 90s prevalent in the Piedmont/Roanoke/Lexington areas and \nreach around 90 in portions of the New Rive Valley. \n\nLong-range models start to show some troughiness moving into the\nnortheast late next week pushing the ridge southwest, and\nturning the flow to the northwest over us. This would open the\ndoor for potential organized convective clusters again around\nthe Thursday to Friday time frame. Still some model disagreement\nas to how/when the ridge weakens.",
